when giving time range 30 min or more it is erroring

When Loki complains that it “failed to load chunk … no such file or directory” only once you go beyond a 30 minute window, it almost always means that those older chunks have already been flushed/compacted or evicted from disk by your retention settings — so Grafana can’t find them locally anymore.

What’s going on under the hood
Chunk lifecycle. Loki writes incoming log lines into an in-memory “head” chunk, then periodically flushes them to disk (or object storage) as immutable chunks.

Retention & compaction. Once a chunk is older than your configured retention_period, or after a certain chunk_idle_period, Loki will either delete it (if on local FS) or move it to long-term storage (if you’ve set up an S3/GCS bucket).

Missed files. If you’re using only the local filesystem (no object store) and your retention is shorter than the query window, those files will simply have been deleted — hence the “no such file” error.

How to fix
1. Extend local retention (if you want to keep everything on disk)
Edit your Loki config to bump up compactor/table_manager retention:

yaml
Copy
Edit
storage_config:
  boltdb:
    directory: /var/loki/index

  filesystem:
    directory: /var/loki/chunks

compactor:
  retention_enabled: true
  retention_delete_delay: 2h           # wait 2 hours after chunk expires
  retention_delete_worker_count: 1

table_manager:
  retention_deletes_enabled: true
  retention_period: 168h               # keep 7 days of data locally
Restart Loki so old chunks remain on disk long enough to answer your 30 + minute queries.

2. Plug in object storage for long-term
If you need months (or years) of logs, use S3/GCS/Azure as your chunk store:

yaml
Copy
Edit
storage_config:
  aws:
    s3: s3://my-bucket/loki/chunks

  boltdb_shipper:
    active_index_directory: /var/loki/index
    shared_store: aws

schema_config:
  configs:
    - from: 2020-10-24
      store: boltdb-shipper
      object_store: aws
      schema: v11
      index:
        prefix: index_
        period: 24h
With this, even if files are flushed locally, Loki will re-hydrate them from S3 on demand.

3. Verify your query time parameters
Grafana’s $__range often covers a dashboard span (e.g. 1 h), and $__interval is the bucket size. If you’re manually testing in Explore, short tests will work but longer ones hit missing data. Try in Explore:

promql
Copy
Edit
sum by (status) (
  count_over_time({app="server"}[1h])
)
If that errors, you know it’s not Grafana — it’s Loki’s storage.

4. Short-term workaround
If you only care about “recent” logs, you can limit your query to what you know still exists locally:

promql
Copy
Edit
sum by (status) (
  count_over_time({app="server"}[15m])
)
or drop the missing-chunk errors via Grafana’s “max data points” / “timeout” settings — but that only sidesteps the symptom.

In summary
Short queries (< chunk/retention window): succeed because the chunks are still on disk

Longer queries: fail because Loki has deleted or off-loaded those chunk files

Permanent fix: increase retention_period on local FS or switch to an object store (“boltdb-shipper”) so that all your chunks can be reloaded on demand.